{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["And the reason for <b>הִתְעַלַּלְתִּי</b> ('I have toyed with him') is that I am laughing at him, for I am making his heart heavy and executing vengeance upon him, in the sense of 'He who sits in the heavens, YHWH laughs; the Sovereign mocks them' (Psalms 2:4). And now the Holy One, blessed be He, informed Moses of the plague of the locusts and that he should announce it to Pharaoh — for what is the sense of 'Come to Pharaoh,' since He does not tell him anything to say? Rather, the speech of Moses to Pharaoh is not mentioned; the verse is abbreviated in this matter, and similarly it abbreviated above in the plague of hail, where it related the words of the Holy One, blessed be He, to Moses: 'Station yourself before Pharaoh and say to him' (Exodus 9:13), and did not mention Moses' words to Pharaoh at all, as I explained there (v. 19). And the reason for this is that He did not wish to elaborate on both of them; sometimes He abbreviates this and sometimes that.<br>And in Shemot Rabbah (13:4) I saw [concerning] <b>וּלְמַעַן תְּסַפֵּר בְּאָזְנֵי בִנְךָ</b> ('and that you may tell in the ears of your son'): the Holy One, blessed be He, informed Moses what plague He would bring upon them, and Moses wrote it down by way of a hint — <b>וּלְמַעַן תְּסַפֵּר בְּאָזְנֵי בִנְךָ וּבֶן בִּנְךָ</b> ('and that you may tell in the ears of your son and your son's son') — this is the plague of the locusts, as it says: 'Tell your children of it, and your children to their children' etc. (Joel 1:3)."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 10, :notes {0 "The Aramaic phrase כַּמָּה דְּתֵימַר ('as you would say') introduces the Joel quotation; rendered literally without the parenthetical gloss."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:05:22.236827624Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}